schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: A Tools Directory for Knowledge That Outlives Turnover
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    An opinionated, named-tool guide for building a team knowledge commons
    that survives contributor turnover. Organized around one governing
    principle ("infrastructure should follow function") and one portability
    test ("would your knowledge survive the tool dying?"), it maps tool
    choices to jobs (team knowledge base, individual notes, public site) and
    names a public/internal/private visibility-tier substrate mapping.
  audience: small teams and local nodes choosing knowledge-commons tooling
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      Polished, collaboration-friendly tools (Notion) vs. durable,
      portable-in-plain-text tools (Markdown + Git) — the article resolves
      this as "own your source of truth in plain text and Git; use
      everything else as a publishing or collaboration layer on top."
    - >-
      Even historically file-based tools can drift away from portability —
      Logseq's 2025-2026 SQLite-backed "DB version" is cited as a caution
      that "file-on-disk" is a property to keep checking, not assume.
  related_concepts:
    - infrastructure-follows-function
  related_resources:
    - obsidian
    - quartz
    - astro-starlight
    - bookstack
    - outline
    - wiki-js
    - gitbook
    - notion
    - logseq
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/knowledge-tools-directory.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

